# PromptOptimizer — Project Rules

## Design Context

Established 2026-09-30. Full version lives in `.impeccable.md`; this is the operative summary.

### Users

Two overlapping groups, both already fluent with conversational AI:

- **The improver.** Uses ChatGPT / Claude / Gemini most days. Knows their prompts are
  underperforming but does not want to learn prompt-engineering frameworks. Wants a one-click
  upgrade without reading documentation.
- **The power user.** Wants a fast in-page accelerator: keyboard-first, no modal friction, and
  visibility into the system prompt doing the work.

Context of use: mid-task, inside someone else's web app, often with a draft already typed. The
tool is an interruption to real work and must justify the interruption instantly.

### Brand Personality

**Precise. Unobtrusive. Professional.** The voice of a good instrument, not a good salesman. It
states what it did and gets out of the way. No exclamation marks, no celebrating routine actions.
When something degrades, it says so plainly instead of absorbing the failure.
Emotional goal: **confidence** — the user should trust that the output is what it claims to be.

### Aesthetic Direction

**Neutral-professional.** Restrained, high-legibility, deliberately quiet. The interface lives
inside a host page it does not own and must never fight it for attention.
Ten designs are on offer (added 2026-10-02 at the owner's request, superseding the earlier
dark/light pair). Ink is the default and Paper its light counterpart; the other eight are
Terminal, Ember, Harbor, Contrast, Manuscript, Sand, Glacier and Meadow. Each must be a complete
design: its own palette AND a type, corner or weight decision, so the list expresses range rather
than recolouring. `unit/themes.test.mjs` enforces that, and `node check_contrast.js` holds all ten
to WCAG AA.

#### Anti-references — must not look like this

- Inter (or Roboto / Arial / Open Sans) as the primary typeface
- Indigo / violet accents (`#6366F1` and neighbours) on near-black navy surfaces
- Colored glow shadows on interactive elements
- Gradient text
- Emoji used as iconography
- Glassmorphism / backdrop blur as decoration
- Every setting wrapped in an identical rounded card
- Elastic or overshoot easing
- A long theme list that is only recolours (every design must differ in type, shape or weight too)

### Design Principles

1. **The framework is the product.** Framework selection gets the prominent, always-reachable
   control. Engine, theme, and history are secondary and belong in Options.
2. **Never report a degraded result as a success.** If the requested engine failed, say so, name
   the reason, and offer the fix. Silent fallback is a trust bug, not graceful degradation.
3. **One name per action.** The product is "PromptOptimizer" — the verb is
   *optimize*, everywhere, in every surface.
4. **Every option must be visibly reachable.** No control may hide choices behind an affordance
   the user cannot see.
5. **Tokens or nothing.** All color, space, type, and motion resolve through `shared/theme.css`.
   No surface hardcodes a value.
6. **Explain in place.** Help belongs next to the thing it explains, not on a settings page.

---

## Technical conventions

- **No build step.** Plain ES modules, no bundler, no `package.json`. `manifest.json` is the only
  project definition file. Do not introduce a toolchain.
- **`chrome.storage.local` outlives the code that wrote it.** Editing a `DEFAULT_*` constant does
  **not** reach an existing install — `getSettings()` and `getFrameworks()` return the stored copy
  in preference to the defaults. Any shape change needs a migration branch plus a `SCHEMA_VERSION`
  bump in `shared/storage.js`.
- **Three UI surfaces share one design system:** `popup/`, `options/`, and `content/content.js`
  (Shadow DOM). Style through `shared/theme.css` + `base.css` + `components.css`. The in-page
  surface cannot load remote fonts — page CSP blocks them — so `--font-sans` stays a system stack.
- **`shared/theme-picker.js` is the single source of truth for theme metadata.** Do not re-list
  themes elsewhere.
- **Icons come from `shared/icons.js`.** Never emoji, never inline path data in a template.
- **Contrast is verified, not assumed.** Run `node check_contrast.js` after touching any color
  token; it asserts WCAG AA on every pair that carries meaning.
- **`shared/engines.js` is the single source of truth for the engine list**, and
  `PROVIDER_DEFAULTS` in `shared/storage.js` for provider model ids. Do not re-list either.
- **No credential ever ships in source.** `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` carries empty keys. A retired key is
  recognised by hash in `RETIRED_OPENROUTER_KEY_HASHES`, never by value.
- **The content script runs only on the manifest's listed AI sites, plus origins the user opts in.**
  Anywhere else it arrives via `activeTab` injection. Never widen `content_scripts.matches`.
- **Run `node --no-warnings --test "unit/*.test.mjs"`** after touching the engine, storage,
  frameworks, or service worker. Write the failing test first.
- **Adding a design:** add its colour block to `shared/theme.css`, its entry to `THEMES` in
  `shared/theme-picker.js`, and run `node check_contrast.js` plus the unit tests. Never reuse a
  retired id (`LEGACY_THEME_IDS`).
- **Saved prompts live behind `shared/library.js`.** Popup and Options call it directly; the in-page
  modal goes through the service worker (`SAVE_PROMPT`, `LIBRARY_LABELS`). Do not write the
  `promptcraft_library` key from anywhere else.
